// A daemon that works until interrupted, then shuts down cleanly. The loop
// runs *while a watched-signal object is alive*; SIGINT flips it dead, so the
// `~ATH(RUN)` loop falls through to the shutdown line. This is ~ATH's central
// idea — control flow is an object's life, not a boolean — and it expresses
// "run until this external event happens" with no flags or exceptions.
//
//   athc examples/daemon.ath -o daemon
//   ./daemon            # prints "working..." every 500ms
//   # press Ctrl-C  ->  "received SIGINT; shutting down cleanly."
//
// Variants: `watch mtime "heartbeat" as RUN;` is a dead-man's-switch (runs
// until the file stops being touched); `watch pid N as RUN;` waits for
// another process to exit.

import number 500 as TICK;        // 500 ms

watch signal SIGINT as RUN;
print daemon started (SIGINT to stop)...;

~ATH(RUN) {
    print working...;
    sleep TICK;
}

print received SIGINT\; shutting down cleanly.;
THIS.DIE();
